WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:08.980
Mikl: We're going to go through some sets of photos about particular iconic aspects of San Francisco.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:13.540
Mikl: Anyone want to guess what this section is about?

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:17.940
Fred Lyon: this is the photographer's friend.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.380
Fred: Fog is so important.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:28.240
It is our identifying natural effect.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:36.080
It covers up works of man that need covering up.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:45.500
And then there are a few things that you can actually manipulate and use for your own usefulness.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.560
Unfortunately it's not something...

00:00:53.560 --> 00:01:00.040
Actually somebody now makes a little bug bomb...

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:09.840
a [portable] fog that you can use.
I have one but I haven't ever used it.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.720
It probably doesn't work anymore.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:16.820
This is out at Fort Point. (Not far from here)

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.080
Mikl: We have a Fort Point beer on tap.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:26.810
Fred Lyon: That's looking into the Sunset.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:35.060
Fred Lyon: There's a little backstory to this.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:42.680
I was doing a story on San Francisco
for some magazine.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:48.740
And I had a phone call that morning from the magazine.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:54.500
They said "Fred, we're trying to close this story but we don't have the fog picture yet that you promised."

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:58.520
I said, look you can't just cue the fog when you need it.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:04.260
I'll get it to you soon. He said, well if it doesn't get
here soon we'll have to do the story without it.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:12.740
And that evening my bride and I
were invited upstairs to our landlord's

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:18.400
who was going to pour us a cocktail.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.220
And when I was up there he was just about to pour
and I looked out the window....

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:30.180
I could see this string of fog coming.
And we lived in Sausalito at the time.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.840
I said, Oh! Don't pour. He said, "What?!"

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:42.700
They're ready. And I said, I promise you I'll buy you a drink the moment we finish taking this picture.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.320
But we have to go right now.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:53.100
So we rushed out there.
That's just above where Sutro Baths was.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:03:06.080
That is my landlord and my bride and my uncle's car.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:13.860
While it looks very romantic,
I'm so glad there's no recording of it.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.920
Because they were saying
"Fred, for chrissakes, take the picture!

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:22.540
We're freezing our buns and we're dying of thirst!"

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:27.180
And I'd say, just one more exposure.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:36.220
I had to take them immediately to the nearest bar.
It was a very expensive production.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:40.400
Mikl: But that you made your deadline?

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:46.320
Fred: Made the deadline. And actually
we didn't print it again for another 50 or 60 years.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.240
Mikl: But now it's in your San Francisco Noir book.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.670
Fred: Now it's in the book.
Mikl:  We'll get to the book in a minute.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:04:00.420
Mikl: So then, there's this guy...
Mr. Herb Caen.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.673
Mikl: Herb Caen... just to get our our facts right here

00:04:04.673 --> 00:04:09.540
Mikl: He started in the 1930s; writing
first in Sacramento, then here?

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.320
Mikl: Does that sound right?
Fred: Yeah, he was in Sacramento.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.480
Fred: And he was known as the 'Sacamenna Kid'.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.600
Fred: He appeared here and was an instant success.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:29.580
Herb was unique in that he really got San Francisco.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:35.980
He did a column that was like no other.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.360
Nobody has been successful in trying to do that again.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:49.100
I was always irritated with people who said, "Well Herb when are you gonna write something serious?"

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:52.440
To me this [his column] was a great accomplishment.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:57.760
Mikl: It's hard to explain to someone.
In a way it's like...

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:01.920
Mikl: when there were 3 TV networks, and everybody watched Walter Cronkite. That kind of thing...

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.500
Mikl: But for San Francisco, there was Herb Caen.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:13.640
I don't know that most cities ever even had that kind of
a singular voice that kind of represented the city

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:17.160
in that way. Is that what you would say from being here?

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:22.220
Fred: I would say that was true. And he really loved it.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:29.120
Mikl: And let's characterize it for folks
who never saw the column...

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:36.540
Mikl: First of all for 60 years basically he was writing for The Chronicle and briefly for The Examiner.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:45.220
Fred: Right. And there were times when
I would be going through my desk,

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:50.180
Fred: and I would find a snap that I'd done of him.
And I would throw it in the mail to him.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:55.360
Then once at a party he said:
Hey Fredo, would you do me a great favor?

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:57.300
I said, sure Herb, what?

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:02.300
Please don't send me any more pictures
of me when I had hair?

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:09.780
He went through a period
when he had electric hair that did everything.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:14.660
And then then he went through a period
when he didn't have enough hair.

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:20.960
But he was he was out on the  town all the time.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:26.700
And when he went to the Examiner

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:37.300
I asked his replacement at the Chronicle
how it was to try to write Herb Caen's column

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:46.760
And he said, It's no hope there are only so many
talking lawyers and talking doctors in this town.

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:49.240
And Herb's got them all sewed up.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:59.940
Herb had a roll of 3 x 5 cards; that were a perforated roll.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:06.220
And they were in his trusty Royal [typewriter].

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:13.700
Whenever the phone would ring, he or his assistant would take whatever came in.

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:22.440
And if it sounded like something promising they would type it on that. And it would go into a little card file.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:27.980
And when it came time to put together a column

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:38.000
Herb would go through that and rephrase it
in "three dot journalism" as he called it.

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:44.860
Mikl: We can't fully characterize it here, but
he started in the 1930s he was writing until the 1990s.

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:51.300
He was writing columns six days a week
(and then ultimately down to 3 each week).

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:57.260
But they were these little, in a way like
a kind of (proto) Twitter thread...

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:02.020
little chunks of [news/gossip /stories]
as you said from those index cards...

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:07.520
It's things that are happening, tips or gossip,

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:11.560
How would you characterize what he was writing?

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:18.140
Fred: Well, they were wonderful little squibs that
he had adjusted to to make them really zing.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:25.740
Fred: If you fed stuff to him regularly,

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:29.960
every once in a while he would mention your name.

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:31.480
And that was a great thing.

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:36.820
I found that my UPS man
would always ask me when he came to the door,

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:39.600
How's your buddy Herb?

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:43.980
And I thought, my god, everybody does read this thing.

